/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(B)=\\pi \\in B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c84b0d0-68a6-5810-83bf-b94b2bd80c6c" :answer/id #uuid "2c84b0d0-68a6-5810-83bf-b94b2bd80c6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "inf(B)=\\pi \\notin B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a321018-3f19-5608-8ca8-df62e8a4da8f" :answer/id #uuid "5a321018-3f19-5608-8ca8-df62e8a4da8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$inf(B)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-622a3a58-55bb-5010-91b6-37bedd13a1ad" :question/id #uuid "622a3a58-55bb-5010-91b6-37bedd13a1ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-253559" :question/problem "Consider the set $D=[\\sqrt{3},4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(D).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $D$ is the {{field-1}} of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a set $D$ is the smallest upper bound of $D.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $D$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge 4$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $D.$ Thus, the set $[4,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $D.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<4$ is **not** an upper bound of $D.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $D$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<4.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $D,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(D)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$\n\nNotice that $4\\notin D.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $D$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $D.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $D$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge 4\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(D)=4\\notin D.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fiel