ction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}÷\\frac{3}{16} & =\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 16}{8\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{24} \\\\  & =48÷24 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $2$ cups of flour per cup of sugar. To determine the number of cups of flour Eva needs if she wants to use $2$ cups of sugar, we multiply the unit rate by $2$ cups of sugar and get\n\n$$\n2\\times 2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Eva needs $4$ cups of flour if she wants to use $2$ cups of sugar." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f330a21-ae37-5b4c-b04c-2851be121d6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f330a21-ae37-5b4c-b04c-2851be121d6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f330a21-ae37-5b4c-b04c-2851be121d6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22d4604e-a1bf-5092-88c7-d8b85fe39f5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1d3ecdf6-cc46-52ec-8cae-496cd449a2a5" "answer-22d4604e-a1bf-5092-88c7-d8b85fe39f5f" "answer-75455607-0ce5-5a87-92db-670bb08b604b" "answer-1d233c4d-91ce-5842-96ac-2ac9124a19b4" "answer-2f5e3729-fbf9-57ad-a68e-33e03b5eb2fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d3ecdf6-cc46-52ec-8cae-496cd449a2a5" :answer/id #uuid "1d3ecdf6-cc46-52ec-8cae-496cd449a2a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22d4604e-a1bf-5092-88c7-d8b85fe39f5f" :answer/id #uuid "22d4604e-a1bf-5092-88c7-d8b85fe39f5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75455607-0ce5-5a87-92db-670bb08b604b" :answer/id #uuid "75455607-0ce5-5a87-92db-670bb08b604b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d233c4d-91ce-5842-96ac-2ac9124a19b4" :answer/id #uuid "1d233c4d-91ce-5842-96ac-2ac9124a19b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5e3729-fbf9-57ad-a68e-33e03b5eb2fd" :answer/id #uuid "2f5e3729-fbf9-57ad-a68e-33e03b5eb2fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-60a4c1ac-7be9-527c-8cb4-fa53e2f8804b" :question/id #uuid "60a4c1ac-7be9-527c-8cb4-fa53e2f8804b" :question/math-academy-id "q-93029" :question/problem "Sophie decides to go for a walk. If she walks $\\frac{3}{5}$ kilometers in $\\frac{1}{10}$ of an hour, how many kilometers does she walk in $2$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of kilometers that Sophie walks in $2$ hours, we can first find the unit rate of kilometers per hour and multiply the unit rate by $2$ hours.\n\nTo find the unit rate of kilometers per hour, we divide the number of kilometers, $\\frac{3}{5}$, by the number of hours, $\\frac{1}{10}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{10}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 10}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =30÷5 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $6$ kilometers per hour. To find the number of kilometers that Sophie walks in $2$ hours, we multiply the unit rate by $2$ hours and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\times 2 & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sophie walks $12$ kilometers in $2$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f15ccc5e-fa2d-536f-b665-e6bdf907cc6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f15ccc5e-fa2d-536f-b665-e6bdf907cc6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f15ccc5e-fa2d-536f-b665-e6bdf907cc6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-10e63e7e-554c-59e2-9d8f-0fbf7c9cf9c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f506304-508a-5ed3-a3b7-51a3ad29b9a5" "answer-10e63e7e-554c-59e2-9d8f-0fbf7c9cf9c8" "answer-baa8c50b-7a63-5798-8a53-5c31f360494a" "answer-60614c7b-2597-5e63-84ac-fb0ceabe8e22" "answer-4d228187-6486-50e3-b187-3897588cc122"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f506304-508a-5ed3-a3b7-51a3ad29b9a5" :answer/id #uuid "0f506304-508a-5ed3-a3b7-51a3ad29b9a5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$10$ kilometers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10e63e7e-554c-59e2-9d8f-0fbf7c9cf9c8" :answer/id #uuid "10e63e7e-554c-59e2-9d8f-0fbf7c9cf9c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$12$ kilometers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-baa8c50b-7a63-5798-8a53-5c31f360494a" :answer/id #uuid "baa8c50b-7a63-5798-8a53-5c31f360494a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ kilometers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60614c7b-2597-5e63-84ac-fb0ceabe8e22" :answer/id #uuid "60614c7b-2597-5e63-84ac-fb0ceabe8e22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ kilometers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d228187-6486-50e3-b187-3897588cc122" :answer/id #uuid "4d228187-6486-50e3-b187-3897588cc122" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9$ kilometers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073976 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-360fdc6a-8e68-5e59-be91-60b011ed6613"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-360fdc6a-8e68-5e59-be91-60b011ed6613" :question/id #uuid "360fdc6a-8e68-5e59-be91-60b011ed6613" :question/math-academy-id "q-141459" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(v)=0$ for some vector $v\\ne 0?$ \n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and $v\\ne 0,$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection and the matrix $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-440926a7-d150-590e-8da5-b5a15d06d323"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-440926a7-d150-590e-8da5-b5a15d06d323" :answer-field/id #uuid "440926a7-d150-590e-8da5-b5a15d06d323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c259be5-acf0-54dd-8223-55148358afdf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab8bcf39-4575-5bf9-89c4-0e0c13cd32a6" "answer-3a373190-4d45-5034-8914-6d455e9c0585" "answer-6c259be5-acf0-54dd-8223-55148358afdf" "answer-10cc0094-bac1-5f58-90e7-0542270cfe41" "answer-26362897-13ad-56e5-b914-73711e4101e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab8bcf39-4575-5bf9-89c4-0e0c13cd32a6" :answer/id #uuid "ab8bcf39-4575-5bf9-89c4-0e0c13cd32a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a373190-4d45-5034-8914-6d455e9c0585" :answer/id #uuid "3a373190-4d45-5034-8914-6d455e9c0585" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c259be5-acf0-54dd-8223-55148358afdf" :answer/id #uuid "6c259be5-acf0-54dd-8223-55148358afdf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10cc0094-bac1-5f58-90e7-0542270cfe41" :answer/id #uuid "10cc0094-bac1-5f58-90e7-0542270cfe41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26362897-13ad-56e5-b914-73711e4101e5" :answer/id #uuid "26362897-13ad-56e5-b914-73711e4101e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0cc9c978-a013-5a19-912d-5fcfcd8af303"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0cc9c978-a013-5a19-912d-5fcfcd8af303" :question/id #uuid "0cc9c978-a013-5a19-912d-5fcfcd8af303" :question/math-academy-id "q-141487" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ if and only if ${v}_{1}={v}_{2},$  then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is a bijection\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(T)=7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ if and only if ${v}_{1}={v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4dca603-a431-59c9-afde-0218f7a30964"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4dca603-a431-59c9-afde-0218f7a30964" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4dca603-a431-59c9-afde-0218f7a30964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce62f560-86ad-52eb-8dca-7d8fcdf559c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-986f71ff-e7ec-55cd-9394-82b48c3bf3b4" "answer-aed3b03d-3ae1-5ed6-9414-6be7b69ae7c2" "answer-ce62f560-86ad-52eb-8dca-7d8fcdf559c8" "answer-dd2b2d92-434b-5242-adbe-1f59d584cccd" "answer-4431191c-327a-560c-ae2e-0a936d7eb7b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-986f71ff-e7ec-55cd-9394-82b48c3bf3b4" :answer/id #uuid "986f71ff-e7ec-55cd-9394-82b48c3bf3b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aed3b03d-3ae1-5ed6-9414-6be7b69ae7c2" :answer/id #uuid "aed3b03d-3ae1-5ed6-9414-6be7b69ae7c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce62f560-86ad-52eb-8dca-7d8fcdf559c8" :answer/id #uuid "ce62f560-86ad-52eb-8dca-7d8fcdf559c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd2b2d92-434b-5242-adbe-1f59d584cccd" :answer/id #uuid "dd2b2d92-434b-5242-adbe-1f59d584cccd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4431191c-327a-560c-ae2e-0a936d7eb7b7" :answer/id #uuid "4431191c-327a-560c-ae2e-0a936d7eb7b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b46dcd91-903b-5d2e-987d-47f39b7bd951" :activity/id #uuid "b46dcd91-903b-5d2e-987d-47f39b7bd951" :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049371 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-492653ce-e0e3-57fb-97bc-5d7f36d893d1" "step-8188ea3f-faad-5417-8dd7-ce49d3aa7e10" "step-604d3714-832d-51bd-9fb5-c20fe1903624" "step-b9c78f66-b08f-5798-8277-4bd48e8f9bee" "step-e7bbe3c7-afd2-571d-8c8c-ff79627d78d1"] :activity/first-step "step-492653ce-e0e3-57fb-97bc-5d7f36d893d1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-492653ce-e0e3-57fb-97bc-5d7f36d893d1" :step/id #uuid "492653ce-e0e3-57fb-97bc-5d7f36d893d1" :step/content 17592186364213 :step/next "step-8188ea3f-faad-5417-8dd7-ce49d3aa7e10" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8188ea3f-faad-5417-8dd7-ce49d3aa7e10" :step/id #uuid "8188ea3f-faad-5417-8dd7-ce49d3aa7e10" :step/content "question-360fdc6a-8e68-5e59-be91-60b011ed6613" :step/next "step-604d3714-832d-51bd-9fb5-c20fe1903624" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-604d3714-832d-51bd-9fb5-c20fe1903624" :step/id #uuid "604d3714-832d-51bd-9fb5-c20fe1903624" :step/content 17592186272288 :step/next "step-b9c78f66-b08f-5798-8277-4bd48e8f9bee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b9c78f66-b08f-5798-8277-4bd48e8f9bee" :step/id #uuid "b9c78f66-b08f-5798-8277-4bd48e8f9bee" :step/content "question-0cc9c978-a013-5a19-912d-5fcfcd8af303" :step/next "step-e7bbe3c7-afd2-571d-8c8c-ff79627d78d1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e7bbe3c7-afd2-571d-8c8c-ff79627d78d1" :step/id #uuid "e7bbe3c7-afd2-571d-8c8c-ff79627d78d1" :step/content 17592186272302 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101007 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dd155880-954d-5531-a956-9c38dd5d225e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100593 :question/problem "What are the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=2x-9?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y={\\color{red}{m}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}},$ where $\\color{red}{m}$ is the slope and $\\color{blue}{b}$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=2x-9,$ we see that the slope is ${\\color{red}{m=2}}$ and the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{blue}{b=-9}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101008 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-939195cc-3480-5b14-b0df-127e5e8e5d9a" "question-6c227a24-fcf2-56a8-8952-2c61a7a9ba4f" "question-9d57e773-1683-50b7-a75b-43650486b617" "question-4f5e6228-affa-52ca-ad4e-cd380e1cf95b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100594 :question/problem "![](images/46/46a04d55edd836050d30a0d4d52c4c441619babd79f93f3540f61a5f437a1ea7.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b = -2.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(2,2),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-2)}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m = 2$ and the $y$-intercept $b = -2$ into the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101009 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-13372aad-9f96-5db2-8926-3d8b0436e154" "question-9c04f73c-b1f7-5a12-9163-6dcdb2ddf789"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100617 :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=2.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,2)$ and $(6,0),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=2$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x+2\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101010 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7322bdb5-3fd0-5378-80df-fffaea4252d5" "question-7de4ef78-0bc0-5a93-a3dc-392320077ab4" "question-e727ac27-525d-538d-9196-20a878c07523"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100595 :question/problem "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(0,8)$ and $(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-8}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,{\\color{blue}{b}}).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,{\\color{blue}{8}}),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b={\\color{blue}{8}}.$ \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=8$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x+8.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101240 :question/problem "Determine the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=4x-3.$\n\nThe slope of the line is\n{{field-1}}\n and the $y$-intercept is\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=4x-3,$ we see that the slope is $m=4$ and the $y$-intercept is $b=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098775 :answer-field/correct "answer-6f1f9105-48d3-52a5-8a6b-793e8c21210e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f1f9105-48d3-52a5-8a6b-793e8c21210e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f1f9105-48d3-52a5-8a6b-793e8c21210e" :answer/id #uuid "6f1f9105-48d3-52a5-8a6b-793e8c21210e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098776 :answer-field/correct "answer-e27c10e5-5697-5e66-9c52-1802c0c760e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e27c10e5-5697-5e66-9c52-1802c0c760e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e27c10e5-5697-5e66-9c52-1802c0c760e2" :answer/id #uuid "e27c10e5-5697-5e66-9c52-1802c0c760e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd155880-954d-5531-a956-9c38dd5d225e" :question/id #uuid "dd155880-954d-5531-a956-9c38dd5d225e" :question/math-academy-id "q-59650" :question/problem "What are the slope and $y$-intercept of the line $y=-2x+3?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation is in slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ where $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFor the equation $y=-2x+3,$ we see that the slope is $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $b=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-352af8ef-2eed-5ecf-b294-c94d703f77d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-352af8ef-2eed-5ecf-b294-c94d703f77d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "352af8ef-2eed-5ecf-b294-c94d703f77d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-402f7c00-2b87-5fe0-9e1e-233aa5e82415" :answer-field/choices ["answer-758b04f2-e5c1-5f8a-aeae-904c59c85f42" "answer-402f7c00-2b87-5fe0-9e1e-233aa5e82415" "answer-2d94c17a-20d9-55e1-9acb-fa976027e3af" "answer-bfb6f35b-2e3b-5ebc-bde2-fce9036dbd3c" "answer-6219c8e0-d04f-53d6-946a-8f5aeec3a9bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-758b04f2-e5c1-5f8a-aeae-904c59c85f42" :answer/id #uuid "758b04f2-e5c1-5f8a-aeae-904c59c85f42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The slope is $-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $-3.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-402f7c00-2b87-5fe0-9e1e-233aa5e82415" :answer/id #uuid "402f7c00-2b87-5fe0-9e1e-233aa5e82415" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The slope is $-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $3.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d94c17a-20d9-55e1-9acb-fa976027e3af" :answer/id #uuid "2d94c17a-20d9-55e1-9acb-fa976027e3af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The slope is $2$ and the $y$-intercept is $3.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfb6f35b-2e3b-5ebc-bde2-fce9036dbd3c" :answer/id #uuid "bfb6f35b-2e3b-5ebc-bde2-fce9036dbd3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The slope is $3$ and the $y$-intercept is $-2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6219c8e0-d04f-53d6-946a-8f5aeec3a9bd" :answer/id #uuid "6219c8e0-d04f-53d6-946a-8f5aeec3a9bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The slope is $3$ and the $y$-intercept is $2.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-939195cc-3480-5b14-b0df-127e5e8e5d9a" :question/id #uuid "939195cc-3480-5b14-b0df-127e5e8e5d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-44286" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddb73fe5746933a3a08acc3fe2a370fbe91a944b22f5b94be1425907f83c0fed.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the line shown above if its slope is $\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=\\frac{1}{2},$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line passes through the point $(2,4).$ Substituting this into $y=mx+b$ with $m=\\frac{1}{2}$ and solving for $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+b \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(2)+b \\\\ 4 & =1+b \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-103e53e2-1064-528c-b885-e2e5926f20a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-103e53e2-1064-528c-b885-e2e5926f20a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "103e53e2-1064-528c-b885-e2e5926f20a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a8dd128-e29f-5f7a-b146-b9a220f5e14f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b7468f0-3052-50f1-9685-8716b5dcbb6e" "answer-3a8dd128-e29f-5f7a-b146-b9a220f5e14f" "answer-58b8517e-abd3-5e36-a214-2c89774f60f3" "answer-f199dfbd-9f10-54a1-a21b-af4b8b1de9ed" "answer-5de0281f-42b8-5005-8f2e-765e65147ab5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b7468f0-3052-50f1-9685-8716b5dcbb6e" :answer/id #uuid "3b7468f0-3052-50f1-9685-8716b5dcbb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a8dd128-e29f-5f7a-b146-b9a220f5e14f" :answer/id #uuid "3a8dd128-e29f-5f7a-b146-b9a220f5e14f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58b8517e-abd3-5e36-a214-2c89774f60f3" :answer/id #uuid "58b8517e-abd3-5e36-a214-2c89774f60f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f199dfbd-9f10-54a1-a21b-af4b8b1de9ed" :answer/id #uuid "f199dfbd-9f10-54a1-a21b-af4b8b1de9ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5de0281f-42b8-5005-8f2e-765e65147ab5" :answer/id #uuid "5de0281f-42b8-5005-8f2e-765e65147ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6c227a24-fcf2-56a8-8952-2c61a7a9ba4f" :question/id #uuid "6c227a24-fcf2-56a8-8952-2c61a7a9ba4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-22608" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a23ec0bd4759cb8fd799954f17df8f9006ee592fbf1f59645b7693e4d9d64f17.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,-1)$ and $(1,2).$ What is the equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=-1.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,-1)$ and $(1,2),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-1)}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=3$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-1$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=3x-1\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5d76f0b-3896-59e7-ada0-814f4f3910e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5d76f0b-3896-59e7-ada0-814f4f3910e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d76f0b-3896-59e7-ada0-814f4f3910e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-27b3818f-6d62-5f79-b799-161420b4f7d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c12dbffc-8810-53fc-a6d4-8d3bcb6adf1d" "answer-484cbc24-cece-533c-9d54-b6fae769d514" "answer-27b3818f-6d62-5f79-b799-161420b4f7d8" "answer-7d0c30db-1ddb-5370-85db-06889107c744" "answer-02fae4b4-e8cf-5758-be71-6c13e5c5c515"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c12dbffc-8810-53fc-a6d4-8d3bcb6adf1d" :answer/id #uuid "c12dbffc-8810-53fc-a6d4-8d3bcb6adf1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-484cbc24-cece-533c-9d54-b6fae769d514" :answer/id #uuid "484cbc24-cece-533c-9d54-b6fae769d514" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27b3818f-6d62-5f79-b799-161420b4f7d8" :answer/id #uuid "27b3818f-6d62-5f79-b799-161420b4f7d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d0c30db-1ddb-5370-85db-06889107c744" :answer/id #uuid "7d0c30db-1ddb-5370-85db-06889107c744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02fae4b4-e8cf-5758-be71-6c13e5c5c515" :answer/id #uuid "02fae4b4-e8cf-5758-be71-6c13e5c5c515" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d57e773-1683-50b7-a75b-43650486b617" :question/id #uuid "9d57e773-1683-50b7-a75b-43650486b617" :question/math-academy-id "q-44186" :question/problem "![](images/83/832d829821a9c18af98f5c474349b09f481c225ec5b6771e0482402d44b10e38.png)\n\nGiven that the slope of the line shown above is $3,$ what is the equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=3,$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,0).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=0.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=3$ and the $y$-intercept $b=0$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=3x\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8abdc2e-654c-5de2-a6d7-f81cec3d235d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8abdc2e-654c-5de2-a6d7-f81cec3d235d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8abdc2e-654c-5de2-a6d7-f81cec3d235d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ad6e941c-849d-5e92-912b-ffb4e060ab4a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23e24a86-5312-5906-8281-44f44127b57d" "answer-ad6e941c-849d-5e92-912b-ffb4e060ab4a" "answer-6a4cdcb3-ac3f-55b1-be2c-56a1e6645cae" "answer-148d50c5-0dc3-5f7a-bce8-d6a5580bf805" "answer-409ca9d8-c1aa-537f-a31a-aeac14b790e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23e24a86-5312-5906-8281-44f44127b57d" :answer/id #uuid "23e24a86-5312-5906-8281-44f44127b57d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad6e941c-849d-5e92-912b-ffb4e060ab4a" :answer/id #uuid "ad6e941c-849d-5e92-912b-ffb4e060ab4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a4cdcb3-ac3f-55b1-be2c-56a1e6645cae" :answer/id #uuid "6a4cdcb3-ac3f-55b1-be2c-56a1e6645cae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-148d50c5-0dc3-5f7a-bce8-d6a5580bf805" :answer/id #uuid "148d50c5-0dc3-5f7a-bce8-d6a5580bf805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-409ca9d8-c1aa-537f-a31a-aeac14b790e0" :answer/id #uuid "409ca9d8-c1aa-537f-a31a-aeac14b790e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f5e6228-affa-52ca-ad4e-cd380e1cf95b" :question/id #uuid "4f5e6228-affa-52ca-ad4e-cd380e1cf95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-22614" :question/problem "![](images/14/14b7f1e0ab3336feee7503ce45b289789a0a9ffc5e2ffbb035c71e8967a30048.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(4,1).$ What is the equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=-2.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,-2)$ and $(4,1),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-2)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=\\frac{3}{4}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-2$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}x-2\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8280e8d-7370-5419-8f80-9e5e211764bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8280e8d-7370-5419-8f80-9e5e211764bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8280e8d-7370-5419-8f80-9e5e211764bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5707be3-00ae-57f3-9dc5-624a663eba65" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ff220e2-4b47-5a44-94ac-9fd5bbc57fa2" "answer-0d38f694-da2e-5bf6-b995-92ea1d0bb10c" "answer-d3eaa7a3-d7bd-5799-8737-e68639780140" "answer-f5707be3-00ae-57f3-9dc5-624a663eba65" "answer-563b6890-e658-5ec8-b9af-0b87df97c1c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ff220e2-4b47-5a44-94ac-9fd5bbc57fa2" :answer/id #uuid "3ff220e2-4b47-5a44-94ac-9fd5bbc57fa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d38f694-da2e-5bf6-b995-92ea1d0bb10c" :answer/id #uuid "0d38f694-da2e-5bf6-b995-92ea1d0bb10c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3eaa7a3-d7bd-5799-8737-e68639780140" :answer/id #uuid "d3eaa7a3-d7bd-5799-8737-e68639780140" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5707be3-00ae-57f3-9dc5-624a663eba65" :answer/id #uuid "f5707be3-00ae-57f3-9dc5-624a663eba65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-563b6890-e658-5ec8-b9af-0b87df97c1c9" :answer/id #uuid "563b6890-e658-5ec8-b9af-0b87df97c1c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13372aad-9f96-5db2-8926-3d8b0436e154" :question/id #uuid "13372aad-9f96-5db2-8926-3d8b0436e154" :question/math-academy-id "q-316671" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe1b1b8f9837ec2fbd6a7eab48b7f4575dd0e247b6d633ec5e440b43f5efc04f.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,6)$ and $(5,2).$ What is the equation of the line?" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,6).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=6.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,6)$ and $(5,2),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-6}{5-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=6$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{5}x+6\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7479d82e-5a0d-5a83-8e2b-a0acea16c02c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7479d82e-5a0d-5a83-8e2b-a0acea16c02c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7479d82e-5a0d-5a83-8e2b-a0acea16c02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eeb8bbce-ec61-5b6f-a3a1-47e0d6917e4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f2be597-854f-5eba-9b4a-9b6dfa58aef8" "answer-1132aeba-447a-5484-8f9e-8a69432727c0" "answer-9c15e11d-aac6-5aa7-9602-3a833ec17436" "answer-eeb8bbce-ec61-5b6f-a3a1-47e0d6917e4b" "answer-8e020c59-f1bc-53b2-b36d-56e3bb5d1e0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f2be597-854f-5eba-9b4a-9b6dfa58aef8" :answer/id #uuid "7f2be597-854f-5eba-9b4a-9b6dfa58aef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-4x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1132aeba-447a-5484-8f9e-8a69432727c0" :answer/id #uuid "1132aeba-447a-5484-8f9e-8a69432727c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c15e11d-aac6-5aa7-9602-3a833ec17436" :answer/id #uuid "9c15e11d-aac6-5aa7-9602-3a833ec17436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeb8bbce-ec61-5b6f-a3a1-47e0d6917e4b" :answer/id #uuid "eeb8bbce-ec61-5b6f-a3a1-47e0d6917e4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e020c59-f1bc-53b2-b36d-56e3bb5d1e0a" :answer/id #uuid "8e020c59-f1bc-53b2-b36d-56e3bb5d1e0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{4}x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9c04f73c-b1f7-5a12-9163-6dcdb2ddf789" :question/id #uuid "9c04f73c-b1f7-5a12-9163-6dcdb2ddf789" :question/math-academy-id "q-171701" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d59cf7d27ed6e7912a53e6ef52a2193f372c15fca3d50272d3c697451ff2db5.png)\n\nGiven that the slope of the line shown above is $-3,$ the equation of the line is $y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=-3,$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-2).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=-2.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-3$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-2$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-3x-2\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2935d3f9-03e7-5bb2-8b88-1d26fc3e9e62" "answer-field-aa9c5cdb-7aeb-5609-a9ff-405bf86bac38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2935d3f9-03e7-5bb2-8b88-1d26fc3e9e62" :answer-field/id #uuid "2935d3f9-03e7-5bb2-8b88-1d26fc3e9e62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6e0afc9d-f9e4-5c4c-9189-e3cdc71ed705" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e0afc9d-f9e4-5c4c-9189-e3cdc71ed705"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e0afc9d-f9e4-5c4c-9189-e3cdc71ed705" :answer/id #uuid "6e0afc9d-f9e4-5c4c-9189-e3cdc71ed705" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa9c5cdb-7aeb-5609-a9ff-405bf86bac38" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9c5cdb-7aeb-5609-a9ff-405bf86bac38" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3f0d1e1a-e15c-501c-9015-e2e2abd1094b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f0d1e1a-e15c-501c-9015-e2e2abd1094b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f0d1e1a-e15c-501c-9015-e2e2abd1094b" :answer/id #uuid "3f0d1e1a-e15c-501c-9015-e2e2abd1094b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101260 :question/problem "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(3,-10)$ and $(0,-4)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-10)}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+10}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{-3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,-4),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=-4.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-4$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x-4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098886 :answer-field/correct "answer-4ee700eb-d3de-5139-9f6a-e06a58f49760" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ee700eb-d3de-5139-9f6a-e06a58f49760"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ee700eb-d3de-5139-9f6a-e06a58f49760" :answer/id #uuid "4ee700eb-d3de-5139-9f6a-e06a58f49760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2x-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7322bdb5-3fd0-5378-80df-fffaea4252d5" :question/id #uuid "7322bdb5-3fd0-5378-80df-fffaea4252d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-179510" :question/problem "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points  $(0,0)$ and $(2,-1)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,0),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=0.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=0$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+0=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-05486e1a-5b6d-541c-8967-f8994e8b4320"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-05486e1a-5b6d-541c-8967-f8994e8b4320" :answer-field/id #uuid "05486e1a-5b6d-541c-8967-f8994e8b4320" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6b3a6a57-231a-57d2-a255-fbe592970476" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b3a6a57-231a-57d2-a255-fbe592970476"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b3a6a57-231a-57d2-a255-fbe592970476" :answer/id #uuid "6b3a6a57-231a-57d2-a255-fbe592970476" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7de4ef78-0bc0-5a93-a3dc-392320077ab4" :question/id #uuid "7de4ef78-0bc0-5a93-a3dc-392320077ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-175564" :question/problem "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,4)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,0),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=0.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=0$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=2x+0=2\\,x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0a06e9b-07dc-516a-ad6a-3fe116353c38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0a06e9b-07dc-516a-ad6a-3fe116353c38" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a06e9b-07dc-516a-ad6a-3fe116353c38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-008ca9c2-dcd7-59c9-841c-96dc71906e33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-008ca9c2-dcd7-59c9-841c-96dc71906e33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-008ca9c2-dcd7-59c9-841c-96dc71906e33" :answer/id #uuid "008ca9c2-dcd7-59c9-841c-96dc71906e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e727ac27-525d-538d-9196-20a878c07523" :question/id #uuid "e727ac27-525d-538d-9196-20a878c07523" :question/math-academy-id "q-6950" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(4,1)$ and $(0,9).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{0-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{-4} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,9),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=9.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=9$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x+9.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ef69642-bcf0-5860-9c5a-224aacb3b1f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ef69642-bcf0-5860-9c5a-224aacb3b1f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef69642-bcf0-5860-9c5a-224aacb3b1f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e045835-a166-5b0d-835d-5cabb6ececbc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34909aa8-cc44-5810-a830-f56ee0fd1048" "answer-0e045835-a166-5b0d-835d-5cabb6ececbc" "answer-c672c777-31ab-517e-9696-8cf1083e0f08" "answer-d730f643-b632-5af1-bdff-5bdd27447bda" "answer-45177f38-cbc9-5587-89ad-4e670443ed7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34909aa8-cc44-5810-a830-f56ee0fd1048" :answer/id #uuid "34909aa8-cc44-5810-a830-f56ee0fd1048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e045835-a166-5b0d-835d-5cabb6ececbc" :answer/id #uuid "0e045835-a166-5b0d-835d-5cabb6ececbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2x+9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c672c777-31ab-517e-9696-8cf1083e0f08" :answer/id #uuid "c672c777-31ab-517e-9696-8cf1083e0f08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-4x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d730f643-b632-5af1-bdff-5bdd27447bda" :answer/id #uuid "d730f643-b632-5af1-bdff-5bdd27447bda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-4x+9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45177f38-cbc9-5587-89ad-4e670443ed7f" :answer/id #uuid "45177f38-cbc9-5587-89ad-4e670443ed7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x+9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102020 :tutorial/content "The **slope-intercept** form of the equation of a line is\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{red}{m}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}{m}}$ is the **slope** of the line and ${\\color{blue}{b}}$ is the **$y$-intercept.**\n\nTo illustrate, consider the line $y={\\color{red}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4c1f53335d7637a0b08f51e93e760d533986e53a4c8896712262688b156bc70.png)\n\nLooking at the line, we notice that:\n\n- The *slope* of the line is $m={\\color{red}{2}},$ because a change of $1$ in the $x$-direction results in a change of $2$ in the $y$-direction.\n\n- The $y$*-intercept* of the line, denoted $b$, is $b={\\color{blue}{1}},$ because the line hits the $y$-axis at the point $(0,{\\color{blue}{1}}).$\n\n**Note**: The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,{\\color{blue}{b}}).$\n\nFinally, if the slope $m = 0$ (i.e., we have a horizontal line), the slope-intercept form must be $y=0x+{\\color{blue}b},$ which reduces to $y={\\color{blue}b}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101084 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186061170 :answer-field/choices 17592186061171] [:db/retract 17592186061170 :answer-field/choices 17592186061172] [:db/retract 17592186061170 :answer-field/choices 17592186061173] [:db/retract 17592186061170 :answer-field/choices 17592186061174] [:db/retract 17592186061170 :answer-field/choices 17592186061175] {:db/id 17592186064358 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-12419829-aad3-59c5-99f9-c3ca68b0aa45"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-12419829-aad3-59c5-99f9-c3ca68b0aa45" :question/id #uuid "12419829-aad3-59c5-99f9-c3ca68b0aa45" :question/math-academy-id "q-75665" :question/problem "$22÷(-11)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a positive number $(22)$ by a negative one $(-11).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n22÷(-11)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $22$ by $11$ without any signs: $22÷11=2.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n22÷(-11)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6d0c77a-4046-5f98-9521-dbb913fd4bed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6d0c77a-4046-5f98-9521-dbb913fd4bed" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d0c77a-4046-5f98-9521-dbb913fd4bed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de147237-ad5d-5f58-b44f-b0ef595e5120" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e4ef98f-abc5-5e98-8041-a38342b8599d" "answer-de147237-ad5d-5f58-b44f-b0ef595e5120" "answer-eb05b152-3028-51fb-96ca-91a12c391753" "answer-17caec73-9eb6-523a-86fb-b27f98488576" "answer-47bce39b-7e6b-582c-8a7b-066f77808d00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e4ef98f-abc5-5e98-8041-a38342b8599d" :answer/id #uuid "8e4ef98f-abc5-5e98-8041-a38342b8599d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de147237-ad5d-5f58-b44f-b0ef595e5120" :answer/id #uuid "de147237-ad5d-5f58-b44f-b0ef595e5120" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb05b152-3028-51fb-96ca-91a12c391753" :answer/id #uuid "eb05b152-3028-51fb-96ca-91a12c391753" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17caec73-9eb6-523a-86fb-b27f98488576" :answer/id #uuid "17caec73-9eb6-523a-86fb-b27f98488576" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47bce39b-7e6b-582c-8a7b-066f77808d00" :answer/id #uuid "47bce39b-7e6b-582c-8a7b-066f77808d00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064359 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f6155bbb-a188-5f03-8345-dcc4bcd6b6f9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6155bbb-a188-5f03-8345-dcc4bcd6b6f9" :question/id #uuid "f6155bbb-a188-5f03-8345-dcc4bcd6b6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99580" :question/problem "$(-21)÷(-7)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a negative number $(-21)$ by another negative number $(-7).$ So the result will be positive:\n\n$$\n(-21)÷(-7)=\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $21$ by $7$ without any signs: $21÷7=3.$ Therefore,  we get\n\n$$\n(-21)÷(-7)=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de86693b-0da8-5680-a7f2-4ee5874a75ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de86693b-0da8-5680-a7f2-4ee5874a75ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "de86693b-0da8-5680-a7f2-4ee5874a75ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8249b61b-93f1-54d1-854b-9e287db3d2d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89708e45-ac37-5535-8af3-daf84400d218" "answer-5bc6dc80-cdb9-5185-ba72-5b0da18b7582" "answer-08c411ff-06f1-50ae-9d29-1123f1bab60d" "answer-8249b61b-93f1-54d1-854b-9e287db3d2d4" "answer-d2880e74-af73-5a7f-8818-28f2c30eaf89"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89708e45-ac37-5535-8af3-daf84400d218" :answer/id #uuid "89708e45-