estions ["question-09cd1679-7967-5d9b-a465-697df00c6bd4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161776 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](images/52/528416f6f9bd58e04afc6df73ddffbe1e8645f1e94fc7113d1458fb806f08713.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-80dc5c66-c7d3-5e30-acc4-5e4e63237441" "question-64ee7b8f-3e22-5cf5-9caf-3d5b7abcf58b" "question-3a0bfbc0-f44c-57d5-999d-8e8824d858fa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161777 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f85f2ebf91733fa3b5f0b6080da27be0ee4333ee2b7b27ab3fac2dad1acb2d41.svg)\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when ![](images/81/815a776cae60244113513dc20183d05caeece6aaef807fd65e5c4ac819f2d4d3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Substituting ![](images/e8/e8a68c7428583d7df9f7fb18faf50f315192475e2f9bb12d051ccc1fda5638a3.svg) into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 23\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =86.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161778 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0aa2d0bbe2e39ba23901b5157466b4099c1f2552d9a34de9af6387975c59e1cb.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of ![](images/37/37cee7d28f0085ee70cefd5cb9ded630e38ffb3ca26523c320df13d09cde09e8.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161987 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-73743ec2-4a1f-56a6-9413-8c931a00dbaa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161779 :question/problem "![](images/36/36f35d430db979991c566198b6126c940d2b5e35682edbabffb458a77b991488.svg)\nThe matrix of the quadratic form ![](images/16/16124e0080293b4221a1fbe668c41d2c293ebe6780025a433530a48ef7047063.svg) is given above. What is the value of ![](images/43/43b267893b1bca2af9d56014fddd4b93a381f8e2c827cb88e6ba2269f1425432.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n![](images/38/38d71b4d0e2e6e4e271e04889292fc77209655fd96accd7ab94358839527c381.svg)\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](images/3c/3cd51b9ca52bf7654f66792b03bd448a08b1122b07fb9028abf3cf9eb317691e.svg) between the entries ![](images/04/041786e547d7190e7dc272dcce677ae110c98a1c27601d701527c08a27ce069f.svg) and\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](images/82/824c1904e6a2dbe749c4a8c6dba76e5f1f3470e44c549f9fe472f11b871b1fe5.svg) between the entries ![](images/e2/e2035cd0dc43c754085c2be1daf4394ebe91d59706bc609c2398323dc160edee.svg)\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n![](images/ff/ffc81faf6d2f06bbe8d74cd6b8fc69e9cd1d38a330f492bf925441633ca283e5.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/5d/5db60b1276b1838de62d9a1fa07bdb79930a293720bc5e88ab3113863282afeb.svg) ![](images/af/af43e412255aa69feb8f127bcba2b8aa43eb5d5efb12d692f4f4e2d49c035a98.svg) and ![](images/18/187bf10c65391a58d6b69f746f7f2c4cf2939b3beacfc02c15cef309875c3b57.svg) Finally, \n![](images/af/af56151e2bfb52d48f7b45d954f8c91cbaa1831fe024245667f4f4d721c87e6f.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-09cd1679-7967-5d9b-a465-697df00c6bd4" :question/id #uuid "09cd1679-7967-5d9b-a465-697df00c6bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94061" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,H=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $F$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $F$ is not symmetric since ${f}_{12}\\ne {f}_{21}.$\n\n- $G$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $H$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$G$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bb8bb339-5bb9-5280-8b25-5509bc02dbb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bb8bb339-5bb9-5280-8b25-5509bc02dbb4" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8bb339-5bb9-5280-8b25-5509bc02dbb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-40bedae6-4059-50c6-8adc-c9c715240d0a" "answer-b7899cf8-c91b-54f9-9319-7ddeff060c06" "answer-77244729-5340-5f5f-9d6e-19fd434da41e" "answer-64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" "answer-6cf84d03-66d8-568a-9b14-8f2ef4741a8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40bedae6-4059-50c6-8adc-c9c715240d0a" :answer/id #uuid "40bedae6-4059-50c6-8adc-c9c715240d0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$F$ and $G$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7899cf8-c91b-54f9-9319-7ddeff060c06" :answer/id #uuid "b7899cf8-c91b-54f9-9319-7ddeff060c06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77244729-5340-5f5f-9d6e-19fd434da41e" :answer/id #uuid "77244729-5340-5f5f-9d6e-19fd434da41e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$G$ and $H$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" :answer/id #uuid "64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cf84d03-66d8-568a-9b14-8f2ef4741a8a" :answer/id #uuid "6cf84d03-66d8-568a-9b14-8f2ef4741a8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162616 :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $H$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $J$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $K$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $K$ is not symmetric since ${k}_{23}\\ne {k}_{32}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$H$ and $J$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160988 :answer-field/correct 17592186160993 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-80dc5c66-c7d3-5e30-acc4-5e4e63237441" :question/id #uuid "80dc5c66-c7d3-5e30-acc4-5e4e63237441" :question/math-academy-id "q-140930" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f799ac64-6ac4-5678-be0b-90e21732933a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f799ac64-6ac4-5678-be0b-90e21732933a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f799ac64-6ac4-5678-be0b-90e21732933a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f20ac334-93ac-5271-98aa-6f85b9667d44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf2388f2-6372-5def-9569-eccffa2b439d" "answer-f20ac334-93ac-5271-98aa-6f85b9667d44" "answer-93089472-c827-5a81-9873-0f6756288ef5" "answer-7d18a3f9-2fca-582b-aa0a-8054fbaae593" "answer-61668a4d-a9aa-528d-b42b-fd1b72e2077b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf2388f2-6372-5def-9569-eccffa2b439d" :answer/id #uuid "cf2388f2-6372-5def-9569-eccffa2b439d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f20ac334-93ac-5271-98aa-6f85b9667d44" :answer/id #uuid "f20ac334-93ac-5271-98aa-6f85b9667d44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93089472-c827-5a81-9873-0f6756288ef5" :answer/id #uuid "93089472-c827-5a81-9873-0f6756288ef5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d18a3f9-2fca-582b-aa0a-8054fbaae593" :answer/id #uuid "7d18a3f9-2fca-582b-aa0a-8054fbaae593" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61668a4d-a9aa-528d-b42b-fd1b72e2077b" :answer/id #uuid "61668a4d-a9aa-528d-b42b-fd1b72e2077b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64ee7b8f-3e22-5cf5-9caf-3d5b7abcf58b" :question/id #uuid "64ee7b8f-3e22-5cf5-9caf-3d5b7abcf58b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94071" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+2+6+18 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6c9a87f-4b9f-58f6-8c95-7390a7567e1b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6c9a87f-4b9f-58f6-8c95-7390a7567e1b" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c9a87f-4b9f-58f6-8c95-7390a7567e1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a222dab7-5e56-5667-9fba-2c3bf4e00399" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4796e45d-7484-566d-8495-7b810c7b8fda" "answer-f712252f-a65c-510b-a4d6-510469142eeb" "answer-a222dab7-5e56-5667-9fba-2c3bf4e00399" "answer-69f280b3-a4d1-574d-9810-c3cd4368897e" "answer-6d75b1d4-e8b4-5c44-b220-f423956a19eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4796e45d-7484-566d-8495-7b810c7b8fda" :answer/id #uuid "4796e45d-7484-566d-8495-7b810c7b8fda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f712252f-a65c-510b-a4d6-510469142eeb" :answer/id #uuid "f712252f-a65c-510b-a4d6-510469142eeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a222dab7-5e56-5667-9fba-2c3bf4e00399" :answer/id #uuid "a222dab7-5e56-5667-9fba-2c3bf4e00399" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69f280b3-a4d1-574d-9810-c3cd4368897e" :answer/id #uuid "69f280b3-a4d1-574d-9810-c3cd4368897e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d75b1d4-e8b4-5c44-b220-f423956a19eb" :answer/id #uuid "6d75b1d4-e8b4-5c44-b220-f423956a19eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a0bfbc0-f44c-57d5-999d-8e8824d858fa" :question/id #uuid "3a0bfbc0-f44c-57d5-999d-8e8824d858fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-94089" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fceae4f2-c810-520a-9489-77125c696330"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fceae4f2-c810-520a-9489-77125c696330" :answer-field/id #uuid "fceae4f2-c810-520a-9489-77125c696330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-912156e2-14c5-5c85-b220-3b8c0472aea4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-912156e2-14c5-5c85-b220-3b8c0472aea4" "answer-96cebbae-c649-5827-9b56-500e87a8efcf" "answer-9f9b3fe8-88ed-5d98-ae97-aabd8dbb4c0f" "answer-83dbf631-081f-5d17-a3bd-bd8aeb056a5f" "answer-1701e258-406b-5e1d-a99b-8123da7e84fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-912156e2-14c5-5c85-b220-3b8c0472aea4" :answer/id #uuid "912156e2-14c5-5c85-b220-3b8c0472aea4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96cebbae-c649-5827-9b56-500e87a8efcf" :answer/id #uuid "96cebbae-c649-5827-9b56-500e87a8efcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f9b3fe8-88ed-5d98-ae97-aabd8dbb4c0f" :answer/id #uuid "9f9b3fe8-88ed-5d98-ae97-aabd8dbb4c0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83dbf631-081f-5d17-a3bd-bd8aeb056a5f" :answer/id #uuid "83dbf631-081f-5d17-a3bd-bd8aeb056a5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1701e258-406b-5e1d-a99b-8123da7e84fd" :answer/id #uuid "1701e258-406b-5e1d-a99b-8123da7e84fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162597 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$ and $b=4.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160874 :answer-field/correct 17592186160878 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-73743ec2-4a1f-56a6-9413-8c931a00dbaa" :question/id #uuid "73743ec2-4a1f-56a6-9413-8c931a00dbaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-94121" :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & b & c \\\\ 1 & c & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2}-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+1{x}_{1}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+(-4){x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,1{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+(-4){x}_{3}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of $2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{13}={a}_{31}=\\frac{2}{2}=1,$ and\n\n- we split the coefficient of $-8{x}_{2}{x}_{3}$ between the entries ${a}_{23}={a}_{32}=\\frac{-8}{2}=-4.$\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=-4.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+4+(-4)=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0a88c7b8-264d-5d69-ba62-b1d185359ee8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0a88c7b8-264d-5d69-ba62-b1d185359ee8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a88c7b8-264d-5d69-ba62-b1d185359ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8d4ddb5f-1beb-5278-b235-e2351d1aa9fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c4b303a-da20-585c-9039-a200f7b368d7" "answer-87a1133a-4a67-519e-80d8-5e89f39e7463" "answer-8d4ddb5f-1beb-5278-b235-e2351d1aa9fb" "answer-96b12f22-924f-5fb6-9f0b-8395ca5aa73b" "answer-eaafedd0-2b42-5f65-9544-d14ab90678c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c4b303a-da20-585c-9039-a200f7b368d7" :answer/id #uuid "4c4b303a-da20-585c-9039-a200f7b368d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87a1133a-4a67-519e-80d8-5e89f39e7463" :answer/id #uuid "87a1133a-4a67-519e-80d8-5e89f39e7463" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d4ddb5f-1beb-5278-b235-e2351d1aa9fb" :answer/id #uuid "8d4ddb5f-1beb-5278-b235-e2351d1aa9fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96b12f22-924f-5fb6-9f0b-8395ca5aa73b" :answer/id #uuid "96b12f22-924f-5fb6-9f0b-8395ca5aa73b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eaafedd0-2b42-5f65-9544-d14ab90678c8" :answer/id #uuid "eaafedd0-2b42-5f65-9544-d14ab90678c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163226 :tutorial/content "Given a bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ such that \n\n$$\nB:\\Bbb R^n \\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R\n$$\n\nand the corresponding $n \\times n$ matrix is *symmetric*, we can define the so-called **quadratic form** by substituting $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the new expression depends only on $\\mathbf{x}.$ So, a quadratic form is a function \n\n$$\nQ:\\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R.\n$$\n\nIn matrix notation, a quadratic form  can be given as \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. It's called the **matrix of the quadratic form**. \n\n**Watch out!** By definition, the matrix of a quadratic form must be *symmetric*.\n\nFor example, given the bilinear form \n\n![](images/99/996c4aa136dd7cda56787a2edb0f9481ed3d9356248a27afcde050ca613b6b9c.svg) \n\nthe corresponding quadratic form is then\n$Q( \\mathbf{x})=B( \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{x}).$\nExplicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Note**: In a quadratic form, all terms must be of degree two (considered as polynomials in the variables $x_1, x_2, \\cdots, x_n$). That's the reason we call it *quadratic*." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162219 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186176251 :question/answer-fields 17592186173764] {:db/id 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ccaaf964-e8e6-5357-a7a2-fd338a200e46"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6337c5ef-f189-51fa-920d-63ed0153be2f" "question-d072dedc-173a-5c05-84bc-fb318762eb6c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-247f09ea-5442-5e08-b3bd-d68a92ad4690"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176247 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $3-2=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173740 :answer-field/correct 17592186173742 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-ccaaf964-e8e6-5357-a7a2-fd338a200e46" :question/id #uuid "ccaaf964-e8e6-5357-a7a2-fd338a200e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-109743" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $4-1=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13419c69-6e14-595f-b394-69e8b9ff2c2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13419c69-6e14-595f-b394-69e8b9ff2c2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "13419c69-6e14-595f-b394-69e8b9ff2c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7112d1d7-2482-5dbe-9b34-f2f88cda3ec7" "answer-d859ee8e-2941-509f-9843-675fc04e101f" "answer-a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" "answer-b461c956-eb03-540c-937a-5468ab8906b1" "answer-0d41baa7-2f99-5aa9-9e20-9c3e4ee48d12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7112d1d7-2482-5dbe-9b34-f2f88cda3ec7" :answer/id #uuid "7112d1d7-2482-5dbe-9b34-f2f88cda3ec7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d859ee8e-2941-509f-9843-675fc04e101f" :answer/id #uuid "d859ee8e-2941-509f-9843-675fc04e101f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" :answer/id #uuid "a1eb0cde-a682-5537-836e-88895a46e126" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b461c956-eb03-540c-937a-5468ab8906b1" :answer/id #uuid "b461c956-eb03-540c-937a-5468ab8906b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d41baa7-2f99-5aa9-9e20-9c3e4ee48d12" :answer/id #uuid "0d41baa7-2f99-5aa9-9e20-9c3e4ee48d12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6337c5ef-f189-51fa-920d-63ed0153be2f" :question/id #uuid "6337c5ef-f189-51fa-920d-63ed0153be2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109752" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(3-\\lambda )^{2}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{2})^{15}(\\lambda -3)^{4},$ the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{21}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}=4-2=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2124c99-e04c-5d5a-81fa-ec9469dfdbf9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2124c99-e04c-5d5a-81fa-ec9469dfdbf9" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2124c99-e04c-5d5a-81fa-ec9469dfdbf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff8a339c-48e6-5319-a41f-01bfb9b556a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea7f06db-99e8-5950-b916-b449dc2e08fd" "answer-ff8a339c-48e6-5319-a41f-01bfb9b556a7" "answer-b4998b58-9a1f-567b-b53e-6bfb546a1bf2" "answer-7850b20a-981a-5296-97b3-dc8c88b418f0" "answer-9df0b47f-d9ba-5e21-ab5c-846c6841d780"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea7f06db-99e8-5950-b916-b449dc2e08fd" :answer/id #uuid "ea7f06db-99e8-5950-b916-b449dc2e08fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff8a339c-48e6-5319-a41f-01bfb9b556a7" :answer/id #uuid "ff8a339c-48e6-5319-a41f-01bfb9b556a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4998b58-9a1f-567b-b53e-6bfb546a1bf2" :answer/id #uuid "b4998b58-9a1f-567b-b53e-6bfb546a1bf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7850b20a-981a-5296-97b3-dc8c88b418f0" :answer/id #uuid "7850b20a-981a-5296-97b3-dc8c88b418f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9df0b47f-d9ba-5e21-ab5c-846c6841d780" :answer/id #uuid "9df0b47f-d9ba-5e21-ab5c-846c6841d780" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d072dedc-173a-5c05-84bc-fb318762eb6c" :question/id #uuid "d072dedc-173a-5c05-84bc-fb318762eb6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109746" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -3)^{6}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(A-3I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(A-3I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-3I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1df4e2b8-e955-5de3-92b7-c0a2fa78d648"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1df4e2b8-e955-5de3-92b7-c0a2fa78d648" :answer-field/id #uuid "1df4e2b8-e955-5de3-92b7-c0a2fa78d648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ee85073-8e29-5a00-b49d-ca1b9acce08f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ee85073-8e29-5a00-b49d-ca1b9acce08f" "answer-4a251038-f751-5a21-aad4-7863df5e48fc" "answer-27c4a71d-0628-5ff8-8f7b-d46974a371a8" "answer-6b88a4da-cde6-54e9-abf5-e9ffa5b03287" "answer-966b8de0-1dc1-520c-ae6b-f1958e5ed590"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ee85073-8e29-5a00-b49d-ca1b9acce08f" :answer/id #uuid "7ee85073-8e29-5a00-b49d-ca1b9acce08f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a251038-f751-5a21-aad4-7863df5e48fc" :answer/id #uuid "4a251038-f751-5a21-aad4-7863df5e48fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27c4a71d-0628-5ff8-8f7b-d46974a371a8" :answer/id #uuid "27c4a71d-0628-5ff8-8f7b-d46974a371a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b88a4da-cde6-54e9-abf5-e9ffa5b03287" :answer/id #uuid "6b88a4da-cde6-54e9-abf5-e9ffa5b03287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-966b8de0-1dc1-520c-ae6b-f1958e5ed590" :answer/id #uuid "966b8de0-1dc1-520c-ae6b-f1958e5ed590" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176569 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{4},$ the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ and the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6.$ If a basis of ${R}^{8}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be an $8\\times 8$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=8-7=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ of dimension $1$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}=8-6=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ of dimension $2$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175552 :answer-field/correct 17592186175557 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-247f09ea-5442-5e08-b3bd-d68a92ad4690" :question/id #uuid "247f09ea-5442-5e08-b3bd-d68a92ad4690" :question/math-academy-id "q-78478" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -7)^{12},$ the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$ and the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=14-5=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}=14-3=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $9$ in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=11-9=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2f04c93-2db7-56ff-bf49-28c30a11d6a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2f04c93-2db7-56ff-bf49-28c30a11d6a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f04c93-2db7-56ff-bf49-28c30a11d6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a001863-7677-56f5-a48e-46de61c50db6" "answer-f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" "answer-13a5a88c-0b5a-5a89-b737-dc9f96f87647" "answer-76c17061-49ab-5782-90a2-1a974e55e8e4" "answer-0b2a744a-7e8d-5a8d-8a93-dc76818c873e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a001863-7677-56f5-a48e-46de61c50db6" :answer/id #uuid "6a001863-7677-56f5-a48e-46de61c50db6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" :answer/id #uuid "f5678664-def6-56da-8fb7-91cc385210a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13a5a88c-0b5a-5a89-b737-dc9f96f87647" :answer/id #uuid "13a5a88c-0b5a-5a89-b737-dc9f96f87647" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76c17061-49ab-5782-90a2-1a974e55e8e4" :answer/id #uuid "76c17061-49ab-5782-90a2-1a974e55e8e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b2a744a-7e8d-5a8d-8a93-dc76818c873e" :answer/id #uuid "0b2a744a-7e8d-5a8d-8a93-dc76818c873e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176994 :tutorial/content "For some $n\\times n$ matrices, it's possible to construct a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting entirely of linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = 5, \\lambda_2 = 4,$ and $\\lambda_3 = 2,$ with corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors \n\n![](images/45/451c66729d04278ec42f1ffcc558245b5cd2ef14b0c3d14dbd35447702b7b873.svg)\nThese eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nHowever, there exist $n\\times n$ matrices whose eigenvectors do *not* form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n![](images/65/653166c6712102e8afacb5e2a73de617a3777e6812d9abbeb760f72b664525b3.svg)\n\nhas the single eigenvalue ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ However, there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, namely\n\n![](images/50/5007b5e49662ccb6aad508b233f4d107bdb89afc2607eee76280a16ec02d6f90.svg)\n\nSince there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we say that the **geometric multiplicity** of ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ equals  $2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177053 :tutorial/content "So, not every $n\\times n$ matrix has the property that its eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$\n\nDespite this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For any real $n \\times n$ matrix, there is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting of generalized eigenvectors of the matrix.*\n\nTo illustrate, let's go back to our matrix \n![](images/d0/d09805a20bbc23954fdec5a893e05ec0bad45a6503e27354df60b4d71952f990.svg)\n\nwhere the eigenvectors corresponding to the single eigenvalue $\\lambda = 4$ are\n\n![](images/e6/e6053d03e5e0f4bc843856d88dbc9e8e14252200a5d7280a4a05872b19bd4d56.svg)\n\nRemember that $\\lambda = 4$ has *algebraic* multiplicity $3$ and *geometric* multiplicity $2.$\n\nNow, it's easy to show that $A$ has a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ given by\n\n![](images/89/890d3b69aa88f0f442760d03afc7333d2e918b5b3401a33b167cfa40321a5f4c.svg)\nand our generalized eigenvectors form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, ${\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{u}}}, {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}},$ and $\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ In particular, our basis consists of\n\n- two eigenvectors ($\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$), and\n\n- one generalized eigenvector whose rank is greater than one ($\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$).\n\nNotice that the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{algebraic multiplicity}-\\text{geometric multiplicity} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is no coincidence. In general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177052 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^4 (1-\\lambda)^3(1+\\lambda)^7,\n$$\n\nand that there are precisely $4$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ \n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -1?$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the characteristic polynomial, the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $7.$ So, the *algebraic* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $7.$\n\n- However, we're told that there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ So, the *geometric* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -1$ is \n\n$$\n7-4=3.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176995 :tutorial/content "Recall that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of  $A$ is a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ In other words, $A-\\lambda I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $n$ steps. This is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^n \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{n-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBy the definition of the null space of a matrix, $(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^k.$\nThis, in turn, means that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^n$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b0e00c74440c269a1d1b1c076aad56dfe3a056a1493c28862e4d080b5c33563.png)\n\nFor example, suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (5-\\lambda)^6(\\lambda-3)^2(\\lambda+4)^5.\n$$\n\nIn addition, let's assume the following:\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^3$ equals $2.$\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^4$ equals $3.$\n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4?$\n\nFor the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A + 4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A + 4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A+4I)^3 = 3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4.$\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is equal to \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^n - \\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176150 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166856 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f1130bc7-7e29-58ea-ab37-f4439fc1b9bb" "question-e121d225-86ab-5e4f-995c-5ee729943442"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167079 :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{5}{6}),$ what is $\\text{SD}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{5}{6}),$ we have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163552 :answer-field/correct 17592186163553 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f1130bc7-7e29-58ea-ab37-f4439fc1b9bb" :question/id #uuid "f1130bc7-7e29-58ea-ab37-f4439fc1b9bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132351" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ae81741-3a66-5168-8794-e804d129e9ac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ae81741-3a66-5168-8794-e804d129e9ac" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae81741-3a66-5168-8794-e804d129e9ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-91460a8c-4330-566f-8171-430847261e62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f92c2ad-9f48-554d-9500-741e357a8e27" "answer-91460a8c-4330-566f-8171-430847261e62" "answer-4821eb28-1e88-51ad-aad4-f7dfdfec6587" "answer-9f1b866a-2690-5cf8-9106-66302437993d" "answer-4a63109f-beee-5a5f-8861-1aea21eee120"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f92c2ad-9f48-554d-9500-741e357a8e27" :answer/id #uuid "4f92c2ad-9f48-554d-9500-741e357a8e27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91460a8c-4330-566f-8171-430847261e62" :answer/id #uuid "91460a8c-4330-566f-8171-430847261e62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4821eb28-1e88-51ad-aad4-f7dfdfec6587" :answer/id #uuid "4821eb28-1e88-51ad-aad4-f7dfdfec6587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f1b866a-2690-5cf8-9106-66302437993d" :answer/id #uuid "9f1b866a-2690-5cf8-9106-66302437993d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a63109f-beee-5a5f-8861-1aea21eee120" :answer/id #uuid "4a63109f-beee-5a5f-8861-1aea21eee120" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e121d225-86ab-5e4f-995c-5ee729943442" :question/id #uuid "e121d225-86ab-5e4f-995c-5ee729943442" :question/math-academy-id "q-132360" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{16})} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20fb49a8-932a-5d90-bedd-3c4b3ee5a705"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20fb49a8-932a-5d90-bedd-3c4b3ee5a705" :answer-field/id #uuid "20fb49a8-932a-5d90-bedd-3c4b3ee5a705" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6ca50db0-0996-5ec6-a159-57c6549064dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6ca50db0-0996-5ec6-a159-57c6549064dd" "answer-0ddd79ea-c919-58eb-b2f1-9dcb609b95b8" "answer-3cc593e7-d496-5234-93d3-64dcd1a6e4df" "answer-06f2dbe7-4d9f-58eb-bb49-d5b0449c485f" "answer-c3247857-d9d4-5eb9-bffe-4aec28e750da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ca50db0-0996-5ec6-a159-57c6549064dd" :answer/id #uuid "6ca50db0-0996-5ec6-a159-57c6549064dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ddd79ea-c919-58eb-b2f1-9dcb609b95b8" :answer/id #uuid "0ddd79ea-c919-58eb-b2f1-9dcb609b95b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cc593e7-d496-5234-93d3-64dcd1a6e4df" :answer/id #uuid "3cc593e7-d496-5234-93d3-64dcd1a6e4df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06f2dbe7-4d9f-58eb-bb49-d5b0449c485f" :answer/id #uuid "06f2dbe7-4d9f-58eb-bb49-d5b0449c485f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3247857-d9d4-5eb9-bffe-4aec28e750da" :answer/id #uuid "c3247857-d9d4-5eb9-bffe-4aec28e750da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166868 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63be50ef-0747-5e60-8532-f2bbd0fe71fb" "question-2edecaf4-e99a-5cf4-8897-cf987c19074c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-63be50ef-0747-5e60-8532-f2bbd0fe71fb" :question/id #uuid "63be50ef-0747-5e60-8532-f2bbd0fe71fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132622" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in hours, between two smartphones being purchased at a particular store can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.1).$ If a smartphone is just being purchased in this store, what is the average time until the next smartphone is purchased?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.1).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =0.1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{0.1}\\,\\text{hours}=10\\,\\text{hours}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next smartphone is purchased is $10$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b89135c1-e626-5a50-b92b-10a362d39109"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b89135c1-e626-5a50-b92b-10a362d39109" :answer-field/id #uuid "b89135c1-e626-5a50-b92b-10a362d39109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7c5fdb9-71a5-5842-806e-387f12f8736d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c341e79-a06b-56f0-934a-8cb70addaf6e" "answer-f7c5fdb9-71a5-5842-806e-387f12f8736d" "answer-3cae4c83-306c-5bf5-afb5-e4bc848532bb" "answer-e5967682-42a1-56b0-aeab-ef5ac5a9ddcc" "answer-89cf7e83-9197-575b-a03c-befeab00a774"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c341e79-a06b-56f0-934a-8cb70addaf6e" :answer/id #uuid "4c341e79-a06b-56f0-934a-8cb70addaf6e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ hour" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7c5fdb9-71a5-5842-806e-387f12f8736d" :answer/id #uuid "f7c5fdb9-71a5-5842-806e-387f12f8736d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$10$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cae4c83-306c-5bf5-afb5-e4bc848532bb" :answer/id #uuid "3cae4c83-306c-5bf5-afb5-e4bc848532bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5967682-42a1-56b0-aeab-ef5ac5a9ddcc" :answer/id #uuid "e5967682-42a1-56b0-aeab-ef5ac5a9ddcc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89cf7e83-9197-575b-a03c-befeab00a774" :answer/id #uuid "89cf7e83-9197-575b-a03c-befeab00a774" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2edecaf4-e99a-5cf4-8897-cf987c19074c" :question/id #uuid "2edecaf4-e99a-5cf4-8897-cf987c19074c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132626" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in hours, between two customers buying an anniversary card at a gift store can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(8).$ If a customer has just bought an anniversary card, what is the average time until the next customer buys an anniversary card?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(8).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =8,$ which means that $8$ customers buy an anniversary card each hour. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{h}=7.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next customer buys an anniversary card is $7.5$ minutes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bae4bea-360d-51cc-8f0f-ab3d756e9cef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bae4bea-360d-51cc-8f0f-ab3d756e9cef" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bae4bea-360d-51cc-8f0f-ab3d756e9cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-87328952-5e76-5226-8b75-e1e77055ba16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ec0aa02-7069-52f5-bf76-34f8ddb54455" "answer-e30bf5b3-8a5a-5ac6-afa4-778b217478ab" "answer-87328952-5e76-5226-8b75-e1e77055ba16" "answer-eeabcfc5-2e58-5fd3-9715-75364be6e3a6" "answer-7af9c393-6c4b-5ea5-8d8e-e6ecae34f646"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ec0aa02-7069-52f5-bf76-34f8ddb54455" :answer/id #uuid "8ec0aa02-7069-52f5-bf76-34f8ddb54455" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.5$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e30bf5b3-8a5a-5ac6-afa4-778b217478ab" :answer/id #uuid "e30bf5b3-8a5a-5ac6-afa4-778b217478ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87328952-5e76-5226-8b75-e1e77055ba16" :answer/id #uuid "87328952-5e76-5226-8b75-e1e77055ba16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$7.5$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeabcfc5-2e58-5fd3-9715-75364be6e3a6" :answer/id #uuid "eeabcfc5-2e58-5fd3-9715-75364be6e3a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7af9c393-6c4b-5ea5-8d8e-e6ecae34f646" :answer/id #uuid "7af9c393-6c4b-5ea5-8d8e-e6ecae34f646" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-47cf4fd6-a08e-5043-8ff7-4d0c5079766c" :activity/id #uuid "47cf4fd6-a08e-5043-8ff7-4d0c5079766c" :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049374 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-3f3c3716-04c4-52c7-b7d0-78f31e2c3838" "step-33d9787c-d276-5c89-8a81-ddb268f3145c" "step-b1aa3e8c-36be-560d-9843-597bdb08b822" "step-034cf39f-21a0-52ca-b145-e27e4f9c0e99" "step-c3801fb8-114b-5472-ad17-7b699337b0f6"] :activity/first-step "step-3f3c3716-04c4-52c7-b7d0-78f31e2c3838" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3f3c3716-04c4-52c7-b7d0-78f31e2c3838" :step/id #uuid "3f3c3716-04c4-52c7-b7d0-78f31e2c3838" :step/content 17592186167079 :step/next "step-33d9787c-d276-5c89-8a81-ddb268f3145c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33d9787c-d276-5c89-8a81-ddb268f3145c" :step/id #uuid "33d9787c-d276-5c89-8a81-ddb268f3145c" :step/content "question-f1130bc7-7e29-58ea-ab37-f4439fc1b9bb" :step/next "step-b1aa3e8c-36be-560d-9843-597bdb08b822" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1aa3e8c-36be-560d-9843-597bdb08b822" :step/id #uuid "b1aa3e8c-36be-560d-9843-597bdb08b822" :step/content "question-e121d225-86ab-5e4f-995c-5ee729943442" :step/next "step-034cf39f-21a0-52ca-b145-e27e4f9c0e99" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-034cf39f-21a0-52ca-b145-e27e4f9c0e99" :step/id #uuid "034cf39f-21a0-52ca-b145-e27e4f9c0e99" :step/content "question-63be50ef-0747-5e60-8532-f2bbd0fe71fb" :step/next "step-c3801fb8-114b-5472-ad17-7b699337b0f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c3801fb8-114b-5472-ad17-7b699337b0f6" :step/id #uuid "c3801fb8-114b-5472-ad17-7b699337b0f6" :step/content "question-2edecaf4-e99a-5cf4-8897-cf987c19074c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064341 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b304ff54-539e-5d0d-a04c-2339ab8e6486"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b304ff54-539e-5d0d-a04c-2339ab8e6486" :question/id #uuid "b304ff54-539e-5d0d-a04c-2339ab8e6486" :question/math-academy-id "q-133829" :question/problem "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Jacob's plot of land is{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "The width of the plot is $x+1$ meters, and its area is $2{x}^{2}+2x$ square meters. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(2{x}^{2},2x)=2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2x & = \\\\ (2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 1) & = \\\\ 2x(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $2x$ meters.\n\n![](images/4c/4c27076821a19720b9038ab64552c85befa0c544e5295f1677b493ab36e6c9c9.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10e8c887-cc39-5c97-a10d-b38457e1a9d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10e8c887-cc39-5c97-a10d-b38457e1a9d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "10e8c887-cc39-5c97-a10d-b38457e1a9d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9ecfe9ff-1de0-5d94-8b8b-66524693914d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ecfe9ff-1de0-5d94-8b8b-66524693914d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ecfe9ff-1de0-5d94-8b8b-66524693914d" :answer/id #uuid "9ecfe9ff-1de0-5d94-8b8b-66524693914d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064328 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-23096ba0-8086-5e46-9af4-3a956927f2d9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-23096ba0-8086-5e46-9af4-3a956927f2d9" :question/id #uuid "23096ba0-8086-5e46-9af4-3a956927f2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133830" :question/problem "![](images/28/28400f096ff0d11258f5aa1f4c5afe7f2189d8f355c520d08b3dcfafb9e8e831.png)\n\nOlivia is Jacob's neighbor. She has a plot of land whose area is ${x}^{2}-1$ square meters and whose width is $x+1$ meters, as depicted above. What is the length of Olivia's plot in terms of $x?$\n\nOlivia's plot has a length of\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "To find the length of Olivia's plot of land, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}-1.$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1={x}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{1}^{2}=(x-1)(x+1)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $x-1$ meters.\n\n![](images/20/20c00e624e31ab0cc4e4b44d4bddf0ec6255fdb674e1f7e2f483b6f53c98c909.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1db74699-0e0e-516f-a37b-e6dc7f419536"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1db74699-0e0e-516f-a37b-e6dc7f419536" :answer-field/id #uuid "1db74699-0e0e-516f-a37b-e6dc7f419536" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c9d1d3d3-6f01-5e0f-a768-0cfa4a676349" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9d1d3d3-6f01-5e0f-a768-0cfa4a676349"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9d1d3d3-6f01-5e0f-a768-0cfa4a676349" :answer/id #uuid "c9d1d3d3-6f01-5e0f-a768-0cfa4a676349" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054775 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6ef18d76-1c69-5646-b6be-0fad6315bf41"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6ef18d76-1c69-5646-b6be-0fad6315bf41" :question/id #uuid "6ef18d76-1c69-5646-b6be-0fad6315bf41" :question/math-academy-id "q-133831" :question/problem "![](images/87/87d9088567f74124a4af55333306d81d27cf41798a5411445b68ae0c8bc57550.png)\n\nA utility pole is located on the edge of Jacob's land, as shown above. The distance from the right edge of Jacob's plot to the pole is $x+1$ meters. Find the distance to the pole from the left edge as a function of $x.$\n\nThe required distance is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that the length of Jacob's plot is $2x$ meters.\n\nTo find the required distance, we need to subtract polynomials $2x$ and $x+1:$\n\nWe put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute it over the second polynomial, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)-(x+1) & = \\\\ 2x-x-1 & = \\\\ (2-1)x-1 & = \\\\ x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c332bba8-52a5-58c3-9d60-949adcb70443"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c332bba8-52a5-58c3-9d60-949adcb70443" :answer-field/id #uuid "c332bba8-52a5-58c3-9d60-949adcb70443" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b1e91889-c904-5f8c-9474-ea776f1c6a0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1e91889-c904-5f8c-9474-ea776f1c6a0e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e91889-c904-5f8c-9474-ea776f1c6a0e" :answer/id #uuid "b1e91889-c904-5f8c-9474-ea776f1c6a0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a9f4b7be-1158-5a94-a23c-150a70b64b99"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9f4b7be-1158-5a94-a23c-150a70b64b99" :question/id #uuid "a9f4b7be-1158-5a94-a23c-150a70b64b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-133832" :question/problem "![](images/be/bef0863eeb7006d89cc92a80be2a84e6ea09bca14cbb29c15af4f2e01e79045f.png)\n\nJacob's neighbor to the south, Tom, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+x-2$ square meters, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Tom's plot of land is $x-1$ meters, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\nTom's plot width is\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+x-2.$\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-2).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-2) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-2) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ -1+2 & =1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $x=2x-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-2 & ={x}^{2}+2x-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-x-2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2x)+(-x-2) & = \\\\ x(x+2)-(x+2) & = \\\\ (x-1)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x+2$ meters. \n\n![](images/d2/d2b543dc559c23c191231543701ffb05a7c8350bc066a047b836fe64766c7289.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6fb5d65-68b8-5e66-b581-58fb181cc889"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6fb5d65-68b8-5e66-b581-58fb181cc889" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6fb5d65-68b8-5e66-b581-58fb181cc889" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ec61a87e-9a96-56fd-b489-b4024081d4b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec61a87e-9a96-56fd-b489-b4024081d4b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec61a87e-9a96-56fd-b489-b4024081d4b1" :answer/id #uuid "ec61a87e-9a96-56fd-b489-b4024081d4b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078472 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3fa06bfe-366e-54a8-ab4f-a6793fc21820"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3fa06bfe-366e-54a8-ab4f-a6793fc21820" :question/id #uuid "3fa06bfe-366e-54a8-ab4f-a6793fc21820" :question/math-academy-id "q-133833" :question/problem "What is the degree of the polynomial that represents the area of Tom's plot of land?\n\nDegree $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x-2\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{x}^{1}-2{x}^{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, it has the exponents $2,$ $1,$ and $0.$ The degree is the largest of these exponents, which is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3fe169dc-96d6-5da9-8c90-60bd8e17d082"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3fe169dc-96d6-5da9-8c90-60bd8e17d082" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe169dc-96d6-5da9-8c90-60bd8e17d082" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-89e32882-43cb-5a38-98ef-9e8ab0f5817a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89e32882-43cb-5a38-98ef-9e8ab0f5817a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89e32882-43cb-5a38-98ef-9e8ab0f5817a" :answer/id #uuid "89e32882-43cb-5a38-98ef-9e8ab0f5817a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-794c979f-c161-5b28-8239-699e664adb18"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-794c979f-c161-5b28-8239-699e664adb18" :question/id #uuid "794c979f-c161-5b28-8239-699e664adb18" :question/math-academy-id "q-133834" :question/problem "If $x=12\\,\\text{m},$ what is the area of Tom's land plot?\n\nArea $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we have to evaluate the polynomial ${x}^{2}+x-2$ at $x=12.$\n\nSo, we substitute $x=12$ into the polynomial and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-2 & = \\\\ (12)^{2}+(12)-2 & = \\\\ 144+12-2 & = \\\\ 154\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is $154\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e136489-d10a-5940-aea3-8d29829ada22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e136489-d10a-5940-aea3-8d29829ada22" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e136489-d10a-5940-aea3-8d29829ada22" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-44edaed4-d77d-5497-9983-08058b8e2c2f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44edaed4-d77d-5497-9983-08058b8e2c2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44edaed4-d77d-5497-9983-08058b8e2c2f" :answer/id #uuid "44edaed4-d77d-5497-9983-08058b8e2c2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "154" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-82932d5f-4a58-5db1-a173-dc03ee3fc19c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-82932d5f-4a58-5db1-a173-dc03ee3fc19c" :question/id #uuid "82932d5f-4a58-5db1-a173-dc03ee3fc19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-133835" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5553b66879dcd4f8852f95d206e806b1ca378d8ca3e33cd9aeb3fc13bd22bed.png)\n\nWhen $x=12\\,\\text{m},$ the dimensions of Tom's plot of land are $11\\,\\text{m}$ and $14\\text{m}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\nThe length of the pipe is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=11\\,\\text{m}$ and $b=14\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n![](images/87/87aee6e1c4071aa7efe82c19ffc6803f8f95a1b4e9108e0844b00593bc37434e.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+{14}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+196} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{317}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea019c4b-31e8-554d-821b-4c2a12beacbe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea019c4b-31e8-554d-821b-4c2a12beacbe" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea019c4b-31e8-554d-821b-4c2a12beacbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-63bc07cc-afae-5196-b92d-d648e0165e8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63bc07cc-afae-5196-b92d-d648e0165e8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63bc07cc-afae-5196-b92d-d648e0165e8a" :answer/id #uuid "63bc07cc-afae-5196-b92d-d648e0165e8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{317}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-693e07af-f6f3-53ec-97e8-593deb89d0fb" :question/id #uuid "693e07af-f6f3-53ec-97e8-593deb89d0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-133836" :question/problem "Jacob purchased his land for $180$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $8%$ each year. How much will Jacob's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $$180$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $8%$ per year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $180\\cdot (1.08)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n180\\cdot (1.08)^{4}=244.88≈245,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $245$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e9fdea53-0db0-55cb-a27c-324b21367093"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e9fdea53-0db0-55cb-a27c-324b21367093" :answer-field/id #uuid "e9fdea53-0db0-55cb-a27c-324b21367093" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-39ff3dce-35ba-55d4-927d-a8dbbe4081e8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39ff3dce-35ba-55d4-927d-a8dbbe4081e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39ff3dce-35ba-55d4-927d-a8dbbe4081e8" :answer/id #uuid "39ff3dce-35ba-55d4-927d-a8dbbe4081e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "245" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147563 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ad0f9fe2-8f77-5646-944a-afdf1267d3bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad0f9fe2-8f77-5646-944a-afdf1267d3bc" :question/id #uuid "ad0f9fe2-8f77-5646-944a-afdf1267d3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-133837" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of Jacob's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=180\\cdot (1.08)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since he bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first two years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nJacob's land grew in value by approximately\n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars over the first two years." :question/worked-solution "To find the growth in the value over the first two years, we need to find the value after $2$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(2)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =180\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(2),$ the value of the land after $2$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2) & =180\\cdot (1.08)^{(2)} \\\\  & =180\\cdot (1.166\\,4) \\\\  & =209.952\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the growth in the first two years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(0) & =209.952-180 \\\\  & =29.952 \\\\  & ≈30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first two years was approximately $30$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3a39b85-95f6-5f09-b603-5917e29c9cdb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3a39b85-95f6-5f09-b603-5917e29c9cdb" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a39b85-95f6-5f09-b603-5917e29c9cdb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-66175cd1-f3d0-5f77-9fa5-d63d6f4a25e4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-66175cd1-f3d0-5f77-9fa5-d63d6f4a25e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66175cd1-f3d0-5f77-9fa5-d63d6f4a25e4" :answer/id #uuid "66175cd1-f3d0-5f77-9fa5-d63d6f4a25e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-1ad50d0b-fe6c-5e2c-bb74-841ddc0330cc" :multistep/id #uuid "1ad50d0b-fe6c-5e2c-bb74-841ddc0330cc" :multistep/context "![](images/17/17918738ad9c2929b01357225553d141a564e8520119f52fc40dfb66020db95c.png)\n\nJacob owns a rectangular plot of land, as shown above. The area of the plot is $2{x}^{2}+2x$ square meters, and its width is $x+1$ meters, where $x>10$ is a fixed constant." :multistep/steps ["step-c18dec1c-3ac2-5a57-a741-820fb6278590" "step-6dce64d3-ccdb-5b38-9878-7d8f1e6130e1" "step-b1126107-4993-5a5e-85b5-f97848cf3333" "step-6b216b92-01cd-565a-9a86-f20d31ef1eca" "step-0a866f97-9bf1-5ca2-87c2-eacd91ab1153" "step-4c43c9f1-2e00-59f2-a06f-585a0d237dbe" "step-9d7cd20e-1504-5f2d-b11b-ef3f790fa7cd" "step-8282f542-f1e8-58aa-a6c7-f27e5ac62c67" "step-b8462290-9bc9-59f9-942c-9a2726b33b82"] :multistep/first-step "step-c18dec1c-3ac2-5a57-a741-820fb6278590" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-c18dec1c-3ac2-5a57-a741-820fb6278590" :step/id #uuid "c18dec1c-3ac2-5a57-a741-820fb6278590" :step/content "question-b304ff54-539e-5d0d-a04c-2339ab8e6486" :step/next "step-6dce64d3-ccdb-5b38-9878-7d8f1e6130e1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6dce64d3-ccdb-5b38-9878-7d8f1e6130e1" :step/id #uuid "6dce64d3-ccdb-5b38-9878-7d8f1e6130e1" :step/content "question-23096ba0-8086-5e46-9af4-3a956927f2d9" :step/next "step-b1126107-4993-5a5e-85b5-f97848cf3333" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1126107-4993-5a5e-85b5-f97848cf3333" :step/id #uuid "b1126107-4993-5a5e-85b5-f97848cf3333" :step/content "question-6ef18d76-1c69-5646-b6be-0fad6315bf41" :step/next "step-6b216b92-01cd-565a-9a86-f20d31ef1eca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6b216b92-01cd-565a-9a86-f20d31ef1eca" :step/id #uuid "6b216b92-01cd-565a-9a86-f20d31ef1eca" :step/content "question-a9f4b7be-1158-5a94-a23c-150a70b64b99" :step/next "step-0a866f97-9bf1-5ca2-87c2-eacd91ab1153" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0a866f97-9bf1-5ca2-87c2-eacd91ab1153" :step/id #uuid "0a866f97-9bf1-5ca2-87c2-eacd91ab1153" :step/content "question-3fa06bfe-366e-54a8-ab4f-a6793fc21820" :step/next "step-4c43c9f1-2e00-59f2-a06f-585a0d237dbe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4c43c9f1-2e00-59f2-a06f-585a0d237dbe" :step/id #uuid "4c43c9f1-2e00-59f2-a06f-585a0d237dbe" :step/content "question-794c979f-c161-5b28-8239-699e664adb18" :step/next "step-9d7cd20e-1504-5f2d-b11b-ef3f790fa7cd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9d7cd20e-1504-5f2d-b11b-ef3f790fa7cd" :step/id #uuid "9d7cd20e-1504-5f2d-b11b-ef3f790fa7cd" :step/content "question-82932d5f-4a58-5db1-a173-dc03ee3fc19c" :step/next "step-8282f542-f1e8-58aa-a6c7-f27e5ac62c67" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8282f542-f1e8-58aa-a6c7-f27e5ac62c67" :step/id #uuid "8282f542-f1e8-58aa-a6c7-f27e5ac62c67" :step/content "question-693e07af-f6f3-53ec-97e8-593deb89d0fb" :step/next "step-b8462290-9bc9-59f9-942c-9a2726b33b82" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b8462290-9bc9-59f9-942c-9a2726b33b82" :step/id #uuid "b8462290-9bc9-59f9-942c-9a2726b33b82" :step/content "question-ad0f9fe2-8f77-5646-944a-afdf1267d3bc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-6c7456e3-5178-5d6d-9eb5-8c4dd7d0a800" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "6c7456e3-5178-5d6d-9eb5-8c4dd7d0a800" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-1ad50d0b-fe6c-5e2c-bb74-841ddc0330cc" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186051017 17592186048928 17592186048524 17592186049074 17592186048406 17592186050396 17592186049224 17592186050886 17592186048650] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-7adb2705-d5c5-58bb-b138-4ef3bb978aec" :activity/id #uuid "7adb2705-d5c5-58bb-b138-4ef3bb978aec" :activity/title "Factoring Polynomials Using Various Techniques" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-025baf26-65e8-5b0c-b4a3-6924373cf7fa"] :activity/first-step "step-025baf26-65e8-5b0c-b4a3-6924373cf7fa" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-025baf26-65e8-5b0c-b4a3-6924373cf7fa" :step/id #uuid "025baf26-65e8-5b0c-b4a3-6924373cf7fa" :step/content "assigned-problem-6c7456e3-5178-5d6d-9eb5-8c4dd7d0a800" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-65c1b757-1dae-5815-b9ce-f664cca855c7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-65c1b757-1dae-5815-b9ce-f664cca855c7" :question/id #uuid "65c1b757-1dae-5815-b9ce-f664cca855c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-149719" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x-y+2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =z+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=z.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42278d90-82a6-5f26-9592-9f5a4df7efef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42278d90-82a6-5f26-9592-9f5a4df7efef" :answer-field/id #uuid "42278d90-82a6-5f26-9592-9f5a4df7efef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1f80959a-efe4-515b-ab20-fae5251c37ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f80959a-efe4-515b-ab20-fae5251c37ac" "answer-783a347a-bf54-5822-92c5-cd38e0951ac2" "answer-ecddebaf-9db9-54b0-8d19-3046d28f4b84" "answer-7a868760-39cb-55eb-9223-ed909c3d82ad" "answer-d3724410-1b69-54fd-82ce-033b21b6c201"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f80959a-efe4-515b-ab20-fae5251c37ac" :answer/id #uuid "1f80959a-efe4-515b-ab20-fae5251c37ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783a347a-bf54-5822-92c5-cd38e0951ac2" :answer/id #uuid "783a347a-bf54-5822-92c5-cd38e0951ac2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{2x-y+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecddebaf-9db9-54b0-8d19-3046d28f4b84" :answer/id #uuid "ecddebaf-9db9-54b0-8d19-3046d28f4b84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x-y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a868760-39cb-55eb-9223-ed909c3d82ad" :answer/id #uuid "7a868760-39cb-55eb-9223-ed909c3d82ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-y|}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3724410-1b69-54fd-82ce-033b21b6c201" :answer/id #uuid "d3724410-1b69-54fd-82ce-033b21b6c201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2x-y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5396071c-65c8-5ca3-8777-9084be368206" :activity/id #uuid "5396071c-65c8-5ca3-8777-9084be368206" :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049562 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-c3d1c306-6a8a-5b40-98d3-f38258a0e7ae" "step-2cc8d661-9c6a-5f74-933a-4805142d45f2" "step-087cb510-8904-5d85-852e-0bf683d473d4" "step-c9a7b8cf-5c66-5737-a760-835d1e04663a" "step-a842b3a0-1650-5294-af8c-a8010e523e14"] :activity/first-step "step-c3d1c306-6a8a-5b40-98d3-f38258a0e7ae" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c3d1c306-6a8a-5b40-98d3-f38258a0e7ae" :step/id #uuid "c3d1c306-6a8a-5b40-98d3-f38258a0e7ae" :step/content 17592186378408 :step/next "step-2cc8d661-9c6a-5f74-933a-4805142d45f2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2cc8d661-9c6a-5f74-933a-4805142d45f2" :step/id #uuid "2cc8d661-9c6a-5f74-933a-4805142d45f2" :step/content 17592186244176 :step/next "step-087cb510-8904-5d85-852e-0bf683d473d4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-087cb510-8904-5d85-852e-0bf683d473d4" :step/id #uuid "087cb510-8904-5d85-852e-0bf683d473d4" :step/content 17592186244183 :step/next "step-c9a7b8cf-5c66-5737-a760-835d1e04663a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c9a7b8cf-5c66-5737-a760-835d1e04663a" :step/id #uuid "c9a7b8cf-5c66-5737-a760-835d1e04663a" :step/content 17592186275235 :step/next "step-a842b3a0-1650-5294-af8c-a8010e523e14" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a842b3a0-1650-5294-af8c-a8010e523e14" :step/id #uuid "a842b3a0-1650-5294-af8c-a8010e523e14" :step/content "question-65c1b757-1dae-5815-b9ce-f66